================================================================
Why the dataset has four licences
================================================================

Every sample is an adaptation of exactly one MS-COCO 2017 photograph.
COCO does not own those photographs: they come from Flickr and keep the
Creative Commons licence their owners chose, recorded per image in the
`license` field of instances_train2017.json. A sample can therefore be
redistributed only on terms its source photograph allows, and because
ShareAlike licences require adaptations to keep the same licence, no
single licence covers every sample.

The release is a collection of four parts. Each part, and the
contribution of this work within it (the forgery, the masks and the
metadata), is licensed as follows:

  Part (config)   Source photographs (COCO licence id)        Licence of the part
  -------------   -----------------------------------------   ------------------------------
  by              CC BY 2.0 (4); no known copyright          CC BY 4.0
                  restrictions (7)
  by-nc           CC BY-NC 2.0 (2)                            CC BY-NC 4.0
  by-sa           CC BY-SA 2.0 (5)                            CC BY-SA 4.0
  by-nc-sa        CC BY-NC-SA 2.0 (1)                         CC BY-NC-SA 4.0

For the ShareAlike parts, version 4.0 is used because the 2.0
ShareAlike licences permit adaptations under a later version with the
same licence elements.

Photographs under CC BY-NC-ND 2.0 (3) or CC BY-ND 2.0 (6) do not permit
adaptations, and no sample derived from them is distributed.

The photographic content in every sample remains subject to its
original licence. Each row carries that licence and the attribution
needed to honour it: coco_image_id, coco_license_id, license_name,
license_url, flickr_url, coco_url, and modified = true.

